From a364014ed409ffe5390a26499d289bd0870043d5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yangtse Su <yangtsesu@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 08:46:29 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] feat(core): span a character box and selection band with
 the font's metrics
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

rofd's page text geometry built every character box from the font size - one size
above the baseline, stopping at it - while a PDF text box is built from the font's own
metrics (poppler's `TextWord::setInitialBounds`: `yMin = y - ascent`, `yMax = y -
descent`, the ascent/descent of the face). The mismatch cut the bottom off CJK glyphs
and Latin descenders, and it left the vertical extent of a box dependent on the glyph
inside it, so punctuation and Latin did not line up with CJK in one line. Measured on a
real government document (2464 glyphs over six embedded fonts, sizes 4.9-7.8 mm): 86%
of the scalars reach below the baseline - CJK overshoot 0.098-0.129 em, Latin
descenders up to 0.188 em - by a mean of 0.50 mm and up to 1.46 mm, while the box top
sat 1.45 mm above the painted top on average.

The provider seam that already resolves per-scalar advances now answers the whole
scalar geometry: `TextScalarMetrics` carries the advance of the glyph the positioner
selected plus the *face's* typographic ascent and descent, which are the same for every
glyph of that face at one size, so the boxes of a run share one band and cover the
glyphs. `ResolvedFont` reads that pair once from FreeType while validating the face,
`GlyphMetrics` exposes it beside the advance, and a face that declares no usable
metrics keeps the font-size footprint - as does a document loaded without a provider.

Selection regions grow that band by an eighth of its height above and below: the margin
poppler's `TextSelectionSizer::visitLine` gives a PDF selection region, so a selected
line reads as one band that clears the glyphs instead of ending on them. Character
boxes and match rectangles keep the plain band, the way poppler keeps them.

Verified: cargo fmt --all -- --check; cargo clippy -p rofd-core -p rofd-render
-p rofd-ffi --all-targets -- -D warnings; cargo test for the three crates;
crates/rofd-ffi/tests/run_c_tests.sh (symbol list unchanged, 77 entries). The
pixel-level tests fail against the previous model and pass with this one -
`crates/rofd-render/tests/cairo_text.rs::semantic_character_boxes_cover_the_rendered_ink`
(the two scalars of one run now share top and bottom, and the band is strictly taller
than the ink) and
`crates/rofd-ffi/tests/semantic.rs::text_layout_rectangles_cover_the_rendered_ink_below_the_baseline`
(same band for A and 中, the region equals the band plus the eighth-height margin) -
and `crates/rofd-core/tests/semantic_text.rs` pins the margin and the aligned band.
rofd-render's pre-existing decoded_limits_have_exact_and_one_over_boundaries failure is
unaffected (it fails with this change stashed as well).
